When I started Frame, I was mostly thinking about the commands I didn't want to look up again. Giving FFmpeg's options a visible place felt like the main job. As the app grew, the harder question became how much of the window to give the media itself.

I design and build the app myself, and I wanted to keep the control that made it useful in the first place. A simpler-looking interface would be of little use if I had to return to the terminal whenever I needed a particular codec or scaling option. There was still a batch to manage, but now the selected file needed room for visual decisions too.

The comparison between 0.1.0 and 0.34.0 makes that problem fairly visible. Both screenshots contain the same three files. What changes is the work the layout encourages.

## Deciding what gets the space

![Frame 0.1.0, with the file queue taking most of the window and conversion settings in a narrow panel on the right.](/images/blog/frame-design/frame-0.1.0.webp)

_0.1.0. The queue sets the proportions of the whole workspace._

In the first layout, the file table could grow comfortably. Everything else had to fit beside it. That was a reasonable starting point for batch conversion, but it left a lot of room for filenames and very little for judging a visual edit. Adding more controls to the sidebar would only make the imbalance worse.

I gave the preview the larger area and moved the queue below it. The inspector stayed on the right. This kept the working relationship simple: choose a file below, look at it above, change its output alongside it.

![Frame 0.34.0, with a large preview above the file queue and the Metadata inspector on the right.](/images/blog/frame-design/frame-0.34.0.webp)

_0.34.0. The selected file has room of its own, with the rest of the batch still visible._

The smaller queue is a compromise. There are fewer rows on screen, so a large batch needs more scrolling. I was willing to spend that space on a useful preview. Keeping the queue in the workspace also avoids turning every file inspection into a separate view that has to be opened and closed.

The tools follow the same division. Rotation, flipping, and cropping sit beside the image because their effect is visual. Export settings stay in the inspector. The arrangement lets the preview remain a reference while I move through the technical decisions.

## Giving Output a clear job

Giving the preview more room made the remaining problem clearer: the settings beside it still needed somewhere sensible to go. As Frame gained settings, adding another category to the tab bar became an easy answer. Eventually, the navigation itself was asking for too much attention. An icon-only version saved width, but it made the categories harder to recognize.

The three tabs in 0.34.0 describe different jobs. Metadata tells me what is in the source. Output is where I decide what the exported file should be. Presets holds configurations I can use again. This distinction also explains why editing metadata belongs inside Output: it changes the file I'm about to create.

![Frame 0.34.0 on macOS with Output selected, Video expanded, and controls for resolution, encoder, pixel format, encoding speed, and quality beside the media preview.](/images/blog/frame-design/frame-0.34.0-output.webp)

_Output, with Video expanded. The other sections remain available below it._

Inside Output, sections provide another level of detail without adding another row of navigation. General covers the file itself. Video contains the controls shown here, while audio, filters, subtitles, and metadata have their own sections. I can open the part I need and leave the rest as a short list of names.

The controls within those sections don't all need the same shape. Resolution has a small set of useful choices, so they can sit together in a segmented control. Encoder names need more room and more alternatives, which suits a menu. Constant Quality and Target Bitrate are worth seeing together; selecting one determines whether the next input is a quality slider or a bitrate value.

That gives the panel a rhythm. A label introduces a decision, the control holds its current value, and extra detail appears where it is relevant. There is still a lot to configure, but the whole list no longer has to be read at once.

## Letting the image carry the color

Once the main areas had clearer jobs, I could look more closely at what was competing for attention inside them.

I liked the technical character of the early interface. Monospaced text, uppercase labels, and blue selection states made it feel like a tool. But those choices were repeated across so many elements that a heading and a value could demand much the same attention.

In 0.34.0, Inter and sentence case let the labels recede a little. Values are brighter, headings have space around them, and the selected control has a distinct surface. I can keep the interface compact without relying on an accent color to explain every state.

The Graphite surfaces draw on Sebastiano Guerriero's work, with control proportions informed by shadcn/ui's Mira style. I wanted that depth: a raised selection inside a darker track, an inset field, a quiet border between sections. The gray palette leaves the video's color in the preview, where it belongs.

## Keeping repeated interactions quick

The same priorities had to hold up in the smaller interactions. A layout can look settled in a screenshot and still feel slow when I work through several files.

