For tabs, I wanted a short movement that connects the previous selection to the next one. The background moves underneath the labels, so the control remains easy to read during the transition. The text and hit areas don't need to travel with it.

Collapsible sections have a different job. Opening Video, checking a value, then moving to Audio can happen many times during a session. A height animation would put a small wait into that routine every time, while the controls below it moved to their new positions.

<!-- prettier-ignore -->
<figure class="space-y-2">
  <MotionClip
    src="/images/blog/frame-design/frame-0.34-sections-loop.mp4"
    poster="/images/blog/frame-design/frame-0.34-sections-loop.webp"
    label="Output section icon animation in Frame 0.34.0"
    width={1920}
    height={1246}
  >
    <a href="/images/blog/frame-design/frame-0.34-sections-loop.mp4">Watch the section disclosure loop.</a>
  </MotionClip>
  <figcaption class="text-muted-foreground text-sm italic">The section icon animates. The contents open and close immediately.</figcaption>
</figure>

I kept the animation on the icon and omitted the expansion animation. The icon acknowledges the change; the settings are available straight away. It is a small distinction in a short recording, but repeated interaction is exactly where I care about it. I don't want someone waiting for the same flourish to finish before they can reach the next control.

That is where the two versions feel furthest apart to me. In 0.1.0, I was finding places for FFmpeg's options. In 0.34.0, I can start with the media and work outward to the settings I need, with the rest of the batch still close by.

I still want Frame to save me the same trip back to the terminal. Getting there has meant paying as much attention to where a control lives, and how often I use it, as to whether it exists at all.
